INT. HYPERBARIC CHAMBER, HARBOR CLINIC - NIGHT

A steel cylinder the colour of old pewter, bolted to the floor of a converted fish-cannery. Portholes sweat. Somewhere behind the walls a compressor breathes in long, patient sighs.

DR. ADAEZE OKONKWO, fifties, silver braids pinned under a surgical cap, kneels beside a gurney bolted to the chamber floor. Her reading glasses hang on a beaded cord.

KAIMANA PELE, twenty-six, a commercial diver with shoulders like a harbour wall, lies strapped to it, skin the grey of wet slate. His fingers claw at the air.

TOBY LINDQVIST, a gangly night nurse in oversized scrubs, hovers at the porthole outside, a radio handset pinched in his fist.

KAIMANA
Doc. Doc, my hands. They're gone.

ADAEZE
Stay with me, Kai. Look at my face.

TOBY (O.S.)
(through the speaker, thin and crackling)
Pressure's dropping on the gauge. Adaeze, we're losing the seal.

ADAEZE
Then close it, Toby!

Kaimana convulses. The straps groan. A bubble of foam gathers at the corner of his mouth.

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
Kai! Kaimana!

IOANA BALAN, sixty, in a wool coat over a nightdress, pounds on the outer hatch with the flat of both hands. She is the diver's mother, and she has driven forty miles in slippers.

IOANA
(muffled)
Let me in! He is my son!

TOBY (O.S.)
Mrs. Balan, step back from the hatch!

ADAEZE
Nobody opens that door!

KAIMANA
Ma?

ADAEZE
Oxygen up to one hundred. Now, Toby.

ADAEZE
Breathe, Kai, slow, in through the nose!

KAIMANA ^
I can't, I can't, Doc, it's too tight!

A valve shrieks. The needle shivers, climbs, steadies. The compressor finds its rhythm again and the whole cylinder seems to exhale.

Kaimana's back arches once, then settles against the gurney. His fingers uncurl. Colour seeps back into his face like tea into water.

KAIMANA (CONT'D)
(a whisper)
I felt that. My hands. I felt that.

ADAEZE
Good. Keep feeling.

Adaeze sits back on her heels. She lets her glasses fall against her chest and presses both palms to her eyes.

TOBY (O.S.)
Gauge is holding, Adaeze. We're okay.

Outside the porthole, Ioana stops pounding. She lays her forehead against the cold glass and says something in Romanian to no one.

IOANA
(softly)
Slava Domnului.
